# Stops the in-session geometry loop and carries its samples back, 100 % form
# (adr/0018 section 5.2 addendum four, item 2). Last step of a pair, after the probe.
#
# WHAT IT DOES: writes the stop sentinel the loop checks after every sample, waits at most 45 s
# for the loop's RESULT line, and copies the host-local samples to
# \\tsclient\lab\window-loop-out.txt with one [collect] trailer appended. Nothing else on the
# host is read or changed. The samples were already sanitised by the loop: numbers, class names
# and process image names only -- never a window title (only its length and an 8-hex digest),
# never a user or computer name, never a path.
#
# A MISSING SAMPLES FILE IS A RESULT. If the sampler never wrote anything the file that comes
# back holds exactly two lines -- [collect] out-missing=true and the trailer with
# result-seen=false lines=0 renamed=false. Read it together with window-loop-start.done, whose
# head= key says whether the child ever began executing. Four mutually exclusive combinations:
#   marker head=true  + samples, no RESULT line -> the sampler was KILLED while sampling. THIS,
#                                                  not a missing file, is the signature of "did
#                                                  not survive the disconnect": the sampler opens
#                                                  its output seconds after launch, so a kill at
#                                                  disconnect leaves tens of ticks behind.
#   marker head=true  + out-missing=true        -> the sampler ran and its file is gone: it was
#                                                  already collected (see renamed= on the
#                                                  previous half) or removed out of band.
#   marker head=false + out-missing=true        -> the child NEVER BEGAN EXECUTING: a staged copy
#                                                  that will not run, a leftover lock on the
#                                                  output, a launch the host refused after
#                                                  returning a handle. NOT evidence about
#                                                  surviving a disconnect. The host-local
#                                                  window-loop.stderr.txt holds its complaint.
#   marker absent     + anything                -> the launcher itself never finished: RAIL
#                                                  refused the job, or the relay TIMEOUT killed
#                                                  that connection before the marker was written.
#                                                  relay.log for the start leg separates those.
# Orthogonal to all four: RESULT: DONE reason=deadline means the sentinel never reached the
# sampler -- the data is good but this collection leg failed -- and renamed=false on a collection
# that DID carry samples means the host-local file was not rotated, so the next half could
# collect this half's timeline again.
#
# OPERATOR ORDER. Consumed by relay.command through run-scenario.sh, AFTER the probe:
#   Scripts/lab/run-scenario.sh relay window-rects-1x         # the probe (measures the windows)
#   rm -f .build/lab-runtime/share/window-loop-out.txt        # the OPERATOR STEP below
#   Scripts/lab/run-scenario.sh relay window-loop-collect-1x  # this job
#   Scripts/lab/run-scenario.sh relay logoff-path             # only now may the session go
# Running this before the probe would be harmless to the probe but would end the timeline early,
# losing the samples that cover the probe's own connection -- which is one of the three pieces
# of evidence the lane is after.
#
# WHY THE SCALE IS PINNED: same reason as the probe's own job. This connection is one more
# client joining the retained session, and a different declared desktopScaleFactor would make
# the server re-lay-out it -- the loop is still sampling while this job connects, so a mismatch
# here would show up as a jump in the very last ticks and be indistinguishable from something
# the run under study did. deviceScaleFactor is deliberately NOT passed (product default D).
#
# Full path, not `||powershell`: the alias form resolves only against a published
# TSAppAllowList\Applications entry, and outside the matrix's Enforce window there is none (see
# jobs/server-snapshot.env for the full reasoning and the RAIL_EXEC_E_FILE_NOT_FOUND it earns).
#
# OPERATOR STEP before every launch: remove the previous samples file AND the previous start
# marker, e.g.
#   rm -f .build/lab-runtime/share/window-loop-out.txt .build/lab-runtime/share/window-loop-start.done
# run-scenario.sh deliberately never removes host-written *-out.txt / *.done (see stage_share),
# so a job the host refuses BEFORE the script starts leaves the previous pair's complete file on
# the drive, indistinguishable from a fresh collection except by its read-utc= line.
PROGRAM='C:\Windows\System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe'
CMDARGS='-NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File \\tsclient\lab\window-loop-collect.ps1'
XFREERDP_EXTRA='/scale-desktop:100'
# Bound, as an inequality rather than a feeling (gate r1 I1):
#
#     WaitSeconds (45) + CollectJobMarginSeconds (30) <= TIMEOUT (90)
#
# The margin covers the RAIL handshake and powershell's start-up BEFORE the script's first line,
# plus one write of the samples back over the redirected drive. Archived handshakes, measured as
# the batch log's relay-start stamp against the probe's own read-utc= (itself late in the probe's
# flow, so these read as lower bounds): 8.5 s, 8.9 s, 12.6 s (routeb-20260918 1x-about, p1
# notepad, p1 about). 12.6 + 45 = 57.6 s left under 3 s of the old TIMEOUT=60 for the copy, and
# the ONE path that spends the wait in full -- a samples file with no RESULT line, i.e. the
# sampler was killed -- is exactly the lane's load-bearing evidence. Being killed there would
# have degraded the clean negative into "the host refused the job before the script started".
# Costs nothing: the sentinel stops the sampler in this connection's first second, so the extra
# 30 s hold nothing under measurement. window-loop-collect.Tests.ps1 reads this TIMEOUT and pins
# the inequality, so lowering it without lowering the wait is red offline.
TIMEOUT=90
